WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.453 --> 00:00:02.790
(upbeat music)

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:04.230
- [Narrator] Short-form video has become

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:06.423
the latest battleground in social media,

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.537
and the three big players?

00:00:09.537 --> 00:00:11.840
TikTok, Instagram Reels,
and YouTube Shorts.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:13.560
- In the next few years

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:15.780
it will be billions of
dollars being poured

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:16.860
into these platforms,

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:18.780
trying to increase their reach,

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:22.080
trying to increase the
value for advertisers.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:23.880
- [Narrator] So let's compare them and see

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.160
which one is winning over users so far

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.323
and which one might come out on top.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:33.420
TikTok was the first of the
three to burst onto the scene,

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.670
but before TikTok there was Vine,

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:38.683
a popular short video
service offered by Twitter.

00:00:38.683 --> 00:00:41.266
(people cheer)

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:46.680
- Vine was one of the
first short video platforms

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:50.613
to really grab the
attention of young creators.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.390
After Twitter discontinued Vine in 2017,

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:56.190
it created a big gap in the market

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:58.590
that was prime for TikTok to exploit.

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:00.150
- [Narrator] TikTok enjoyed rapid growth

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:02.220
in places like the US and India,

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:04.050
and by February 2019

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.660
the app had been downloaded
more than a billion times

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:08.400
on Apple iOS and Android

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.980
according to app analytics
site Sensor Tower.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:14.550
But in 2020, the narrative
started to change.

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:16.710
- America and other
countries went into lockdown

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:17.970
because of the coronavirus,

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:20.340
people started spending
more time on TikTok,

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:22.860
and all of a sudden it seemed like

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.170
the scrutiny on TikTok also increased.

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.210
- [Narrator] President
Donald Trump voiced concern

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:29.760
over the possibility
that user data on TikTok

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:31.530
could be shared with
the Chinese government

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:33.900
through the app's parent
company ByteDance.

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:36.930
- We're looking at TikTok,
we may be banning TikTok.

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:40.230
- He launched a lengthy back and forth

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:42.930
with TikTok and its
Chinese parent ByteDance

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:46.590
that never really reached
resolution during his presidency

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:49.410
but had a really huge destabilizing effect

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:53.036
on the app's position in the US.

00:01:53.036 --> 00:01:54.990
- [Narrator] At the time,
TikTok officials said

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:58.350
that user data was held in
servers in the US and Singapore

00:01:58.350 --> 00:01:59.910
and wouldn't be shared.

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:03.120
In June 2020, India decided to ban TikTok.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:06.660
- That's important
because it presented a gap

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:11.340
for rivals like Facebook
and Google's YouTube

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:15.420
to try to offer their own
products in the market.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:17.460
- [Narrator] And sure
enough, in late 2020,

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:18.930
Facebook and YouTube launched

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:21.240
their own short video services.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:23.730
Snapchat also entered
the mix with Spotlight

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:26.610
which offers a daily pool of
more than one million dollars

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:29.910
to users who create the
most entertaining Snaps.

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:34.910
- Instagram and YouTube already
had large built-in audiences

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.410
and TikTok had primed people to want

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:38.940
this kind of short-form video.

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:40.980
When Instagram and YouTube
started rolling out

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:42.180
their own services,

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:44.250
they took off pretty quickly.

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:46.530
- [Narrator] Meta hasn't
said how many monthly users

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:47.670
interact with Reels,

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:48.900
but Instagram passed

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:51.360
two billion monthly
active users last year,

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.010
and in its April earnings call

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:54.967
Chief Executive Mark Zuckerberg said,

00:02:54.967 --> 00:02:59.017
"Reels already makes up
more than 20% of the time

00:02:59.017 --> 00:03:00.930
"that people spend on Instagram."

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:02.190
- [Narrator] Google said in June

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:04.530
that more than 1.5 billion people

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:06.450
watch YouTube Shorts every month.

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:08.550
TikTok said in September the app had drawn

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:10.650
more than one billion monthly users.

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:12.630
Third party estimates
have shown that number

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:15.483
as high as 1.6 billion monthly users.

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:19.140
The short video craze
also inspired Pinterest

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:20.790
to create Watch last year,

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:23.850
where users can scroll through
short videos and pictures.

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:26.610
Netflix launched a
feature called Fast Laughs

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:29.013
that serves up short
clips from Netflix shows.

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:31.740
And even Twitter said in December

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:34.140
that it was testing out
an updated Explore tab

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:37.263
to allow users to scroll
through short-form video tweets.

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:39.990
So with all these options,

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:43.260
which platform is the
most popular among users?

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:45.720
- TikTok certainly seems
like is still in the lead.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:47.100
- [Narrator] If you look at the user data

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:49.470
from digital intelligence
company SimilarWeb,

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:51.592
people say they're spending
more time on TikTok

00:03:51.592 --> 00:03:54.060
than on the Instagram
app or YouTube platform.

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:55.020
- With that being said,

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:58.680
Reels and Shorts are gaining very quickly.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:03:59.700
- [Narrator] In a June survey

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:02.340
by data platform company
Inmar Intelligence,

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:04.530
44% of people picked TikTok

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:06.840
as their preferred
short-form video service,

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:09.300
followed by 29% for YouTube Shorts

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:12.120
and 20% for Instagram Reels.

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:14.470
Another measure of success
for these platforms?

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:17.550
Advertising revenue.
- TikTok seems to be

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:22.080
the farthest along in turning
on advertising on the service.

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:25.830
This year, they're projected
to make about $12 billion

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:27.120
in advertising revenue,

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:29.428
up from $4 billion last year.

00:04:29.428 --> 00:04:30.261
- [Narrator] TikTok partners

00:04:30.261 --> 00:04:32.250
with e-commerce platforms like Shopify

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:34.260
to help businesses advertise directly

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:36.060
through their Shopify dashboards.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:37.140
And earlier this year,

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:39.600
in an effort to attract more advertisers,

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:41.970
TikTok enabled first
and third party cookies

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:43.860
on top of its existing tools.

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:46.350
- YouTube Shorts only recently started

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:48.300
allowing ads on the service.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:51.970
Reels is also more immature

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:54.630
turning on ads on the service.

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:56.220
- [Narrator] But they're
quickly catching up.

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:58.140
Their parent companies, Google and Meta,

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:00.750
are two of the largest
online advertising companies

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:01.620
in the world.

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:03.270
- It's a fairly safe assumption

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:05.280
that they're putting a lot of effort

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:08.460
behind getting digital
ads on these services

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:11.730
and making that a bigger part

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:15.120
of their growth story for the future.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:17.070
- [Narrator] Second place
is still a close battle,

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:19.890
but it appears that Shorts
is gaining fast on TikTok

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:22.710
based on viewer data
Google released in June.

00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:25.560
Shorts began rolling
out ads globally in May

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:27.960
and Google said it would
eventually allow companies

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:28.897
to make their ads shoppable,

00:05:28.897 --> 00:05:31.290
like on TikTok and Reels.

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:34.740
- It's still a little bit early
to tell how that is going,

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:38.550
but YouTube right now is looking
for a new source of growth

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:40.260
as revenue growth has been flagging,

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:43.560
so they're putting a lot
of work into making Shorts

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:45.420
a big advertisement platform.

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:47.970
- [Narrator] YouTube Shorts
also has a unique advantage.

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:50.670
It allows creators to
use the analytics tools

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:52.200
on YouTube's main platform,

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:55.050
which provide data about
who is watching videos.

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:57.420
These tools help creators work with brands

00:05:57.420 --> 00:06:00.270
and prove the effectiveness
of their sponsored content,

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:02.310
but YouTube Shorts doesn't offer creators

00:06:02.310 --> 00:06:04.950
the same special effects
options as TikTok.

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:07.080
This spring, it rolled out new features,

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:08.640
including one that lets users

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:11.670
turn snippets of existing
YouTube videos into Shorts,

00:06:11.670 --> 00:06:13.713
but it's still missing tools like this,

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:16.590
which lets creators combine their video

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:18.213
with one from another user.

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:23.040
It's harder to pinpoint
where Instagram Reels is

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:23.873
in this race

00:06:23.873 --> 00:06:25.980
because Meta has been
reluctant to release data

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:28.050
about monthly active users on Reels.

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:29.790
But in its July earnings call,

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:32.437
Chief Executive Mark
Zuckerberg said the company saw

00:06:32.437 --> 00:06:34.297
"a more than 30% increase

00:06:34.297 --> 00:06:37.057
"in the time that people
spend engaging with Reels

00:06:37.057 --> 00:06:38.790
"across Facebook and Instagram."

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:40.440
- [Narrator] However,
this is the first time

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:43.080
that Meta has ever reported
a decline in revenue,

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:45.900
down almost 1% from the previous year.

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:49.230
Earlier this month, Meta said
it is reallocating resources

00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:52.260
from its Facebook News tab and
newsletter platform Bulletin

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:53.700
as part of a broader shift

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:55.680
toward short-form video content creators

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:57.180
that can compete with TikTok.

00:06:57.180 --> 00:06:59.430
The company also said Facebook's Home feed

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:00.810
will be more personalized,

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:02.820
featuring Reels and content driven

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:04.860
by the platform's algorithm.

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:06.120
As the race continues,

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:09.000
TikTok remains a concern
for some US lawmakers

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:11.430
and users concerned about data privacy,

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:14.370
but the company has taken
steps to address this.

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:18.240
In June, the company said
that 100% of US user traffic

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:20.370
was being routed to its partner Oracle,

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:22.440
and TikTok said that while it still uses

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:25.200
its own US and Singapore
data centers as backup,

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:28.620
it expects to delete US user
data from its own data centers

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:31.110
and migrate fully to Oracle servers.

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:34.350
But for now, TikTok's algorithm
still seems to attract

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:36.660
more users among the
short video platforms,

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:40.462
and that might help it remain
the front runner in this race.

00:07:40.462 --> 00:07:43.045
(gentle music)

